    19	#      * Original: a left frame could emit east while a distant top frame emits north;
    20	#        if their paths miss, the output is just two unrelated lines.
    21	#      * Combo 90-degree case: the left frame emits east and the lower frame emits
    22	#        north into the same meeting cell. That cell stays blank, then both traces
    23	#        turn into a shared diagonal direction.
    24	#      * Combo 45-degree case: a left frame emits east and a lower-left frame emits
    25	#        north-east. They meet in one cell, keep that cell filled, and continue as a
    26	#        zigzag band.
    27	#      The visible difference from the base task is that source counting and free
    28	#      layout disappear; the collision aftermath becomes the only meaningful clue.
    29	#
    30	# 2. Complexity calibration:
    31	#    - The input domain is intentionally reduced to two single-gap frames, but not to
    32	#      one fixed collision outcome. We keep two collision families from the original
    33	#      logic: 90-degree (blank junction) and 45-degree (gapless zigzag).
    34	#    - This keeps the variant visually distinct and avoids trivializing it into
    35	#      "always draw one fixed diagonal" while still preventing the generator from
    36	#      producing messy many-beam tangles.
